  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 240 AM MDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\nIt looks like quite an active few days across the area as my \nnemesis, the monsoon, with an assist from any upper level low, will \ncontinue to bring rounds of showers and thunderstorms for the next \nfew days. We continue to have convection tonight, especially in \nwestern Wyoming where some storms have been quite strong. They \nshould slowly weaken as we head into the night. However, many of the \nstorms have produce very rain back in Idaho and some local flooding \nwill be possible before sunrise, especially north of Jackson and \ninto Yellowstone Park.\n\nAs for today, some showers and an isolated thunderstorm will be \naround in the morning. The main action looks to be this afternoon \nand tonight though as we get some daytime heating to destabilize the \natmosphere and a shortwave around the parent upper level low and \nbrings some additional lift in the afternoon and evening. The result \nwill be a good coverage of showers and thunderstorms across the \narea. As for timing, the most numerous storms should be west of the \nDivide and across southern Wyoming in the afternoon, then moving \nEast of the Divide later in the afternoon and into the evening. \nThere is fair model agreement in the most numerous storms to be in \nthe northern counties East of the Divide this evening, with \nlessening chances further south. This lines up well with the flood \nwatch, which we will continue as is. Precipitable water values \nremain abnormally high, generally 150 to as much as 200 percent of \nnormal, especially East of the Divide. So, as would be expected, any \nstorm could produce heavy rain and local flooding. But there is \nanother aspect. There could be some strong to possible severe \nthunderstorms. There is close to 1000 J/Kg of mid level CAPE as the \ntrough approaches the western portions of the state. There is \nobviously plenty of moisture. Directional shear will also be \nincreasing as the upper level low moves closer. The main threat \nother than heavy rain would be hail, especially with fairly low wet \nbulb zero levels. Showers and storms should continue into the night, \nalthough coverage should gradually decrease.\n\nThe upper level low continues to drift eastward on Saturday, moving \ninto Wyoming later in the day. On this day, the most numerous \nshowers and storms will shift to northern Wyoming. Some drier air \nmay try to wrap into the southern portions of the area, possibly \nshutting of convection for a while. We have maintained at least a 1 \nin 5 chance for all areas though. A better chance of showers likely \nreturns Saturday night though as low pressure moves across the area. \nAgain, despite decent steering flow, the amount of moisture will be \nsufficient for locally heavy rain and possible flooding.\n\nAs we head into Sunday, conditions may begin to improve as the low \nmoves east into the Dakotas. There will still be some showers around \nfrom the wrap around moisture though. The main threat on this day \nshifts to northeastern portions of the area, with decreasing chances \nto the south and west, with mainly dry conditions expected across \nsouthwestern Wyoming. With flow turning northwest, it should be a \ncooler day as well. Showers should then end for all areas by Sunday \nevening.\n\nThere continues to be more uncertainty regarding next week. Some \nguidance brings another system up from the southwest with another \nchance of showers on Monday or Tuesday, others keep it south of the \narea, primarily impacting Utah and Colorado. Given the concerns in \nthe short term, we didn't have time to take a good look this far \nout. One thing are more confident is that flow will turn south to \nsouthwesterly again, and bring another period of above normal \ntemperatures for the middle and end of next week.",
